        "content": "Read one document and report what it says in answer to a question. Use only this document: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no guesses.\n\nQuestion: What is the occupation of Adriano Reginato?\n\nDocument:\nAdriano Reginato\n\nAdriano Reginato is an Italian Catholic bishop and former professional goalkeeper. Born on August 22, 1966, in Milan, Italy, Reginato has led a remarkable dual career, excelling in both the world of sports and the Catholic Church. Early Life and Football Career\n\nReginato began his football career at a young age, playing for local clubs in Milan before joining the Italian Serie A team, Inter Milan, in 1985. He went on to play for several other teams, including AC Milan and Juventus, earning a reputation as a skilled and agile goalkeeper. Reginato's most notable achievement in football was winning the 1990 FIFA World Cup with the Italian national team. Studies and Ordination\n\nIn 1995, Reginato began to feel a calling to the priesthood and enrolled in the Pontifical Lateran University in Rome, where he studied theology and philosophy. He was ordained as a Catholic priest in 2001 and went on to serve in various parishes throughout Italy. Episcopal Career\n\nIn 2010, Reginato was appointed as the Auxiliary Bishop of Milan by Pope Benedict XVI. He was consecrated as a bishop on May 15, 2010, at the Milan Cathedral. Reginato's episcopal motto, \"Fides et Fortitudo\" (Faith and Strength), reflects his commitment to spreading the Gospel and serving the faithful. In 2015, Reginato was appointed as the Bishop of the Diocese of Pavia, where he has continued to serve the Catholic community with dedication and compassion. References\n\n* \"Adriano Reginato: From Goalkeeper to Bishop\" by Alessandro Bianchi, La Repubblica, May 16, 2010\n* \"The Unlikely Bishop: Adriano Reginato's Journey\" by Marco Politi, The Tablet, June 2015\n* \"Reginato, Adriano\" in The Catholic Hierarchy, David M. Cheney, 2020\n\nStudies\n\n* \"The Intersection of Faith and Sports: A Case Study of Adriano Reginato\" by Dr. Maria Rossi, Journal of Sports and Christianity, Vol. 10, No. 2, 2017\n* \"From the Pitch to the Pulpit: The Dual Career of Adriano Reginato\" by Dr. Luca Bianchi, International Journal of Sports Ministry, Vol. 12, No.\n\nReply with JSON only, in this form:\n{\"relevant\": true, \"cards\": [{\"entity\": \"\", \"sense\": \"\", \"answer\": \"\", \"as_of\": \"\", \"claim_kind\": \"\", \"attributed_to\": \"\", \"evidence\": \"\", \"claim\": \"\"}]}\n\n\"relevant\": false if the document is about some other topic and is of no use for the question; then \"cards\" is [].\n\"cards\": one card for each answer the document itself gives to the question. Usually that is one card. It is several cards if the document gives several answers, such as a list of names: one card each. It is [] if the document gives no answer. The question may fit several things that share a name: whichever of them this document is about, report its answer. If the document only says that some answer is wrong, write no card for that answer.\n\nThe fields of a card:\n- \"entity\": who or what this answer is about, as specifically as the document identifies it: the full name, plus the details the document gives that tell it apart from others with the same name (what kind of thing it is, profession or role, nationality or place, years, creator, other names), even where the question does not ask about them. At most about 25 words.\n- \"sense\": which event, edition, version, condition, place or measure the answer refers to, if the document says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"answer\": the answer, copied from the document as it is written there, and short: a full name, a date, a number with its unit, or a short phrase. For a yes-or-no question, \"yes\" or \"no\".\n- \"as_of\": the date at which this was true, if the document says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"claim_kind\": \"factual\", or \"opinion\" for a judgment, a recommendation or a contested finding.\n- \"attributed_to\": who or what the document says this comes from, such as a census, a study or a named person, if it says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"evidence\": the sentence of the document that gives the answer, copied word for word. Two sentences if one is not enough.\n- \"claim\": one sentence that states the answer on its own and names the entity.\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
      }
